title = "Three FreeBSD boxes instead of a k3s cluster"
date = 2026-08-04
description = "I run FreeBSD in production and I also wrote the k3s kit, so this is an argument with myself. Three jailed boxes behind a load balancer cost a fraction of a three node cluster and give up the scheduler, the rollout and the admission control. Here is where I think the line actually falls, with the measured numbers from both sides."
series = "self-hosting"
part = 4
+++

I published [a k3s platform kit](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/) with the bill attached,
and I run [a production app on one FreeBSD box](/blog/athletos-freebsd/) for
about €7 a month. Both are mine. They disagree.

So here is the question I keep not answering: for the shape of workload most
people actually have — a handful of services, one operator, a private network —
is a three node k3s cluster better than three FreeBSD boxes running jails behind
a load balancer?

I do not think it is. I think the crossover is much further out than the
industry default assumes, and I think I can say roughly where it is. I also
think the FreeBSD answer loses badly past that point, and not for the reasons
people usually give.

::: note
**Part 4 of 7 of [Running things on your own hardware](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about building a platform on your own hardware and then arguing about whether to.

Previous: [The edge of a self-hosted cluster](/blog/the-edge-of-a-self-hosted-cluster/)

